WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.360
In Chapter 1, the GPU doubles five input values.

00:04.080 --> 00:05.080
Let's follow one of them.

00:05.500 --> 00:07.500
1 .0 becomes 2 .0.

00:08.100 --> 00:13.180
The GPU program supplies instructions, individual
operations such as a load, a

00:13.180 --> 00:14.460
multiply, or a store.

00:15.180 --> 00:19.760
A streaming multiprocessor, or SM, provides the hardware
that performs those

00:19.760 --> 00:20.340
instructions.

00:21.180 --> 00:25.180
The input and output arrays are in GPU memory outside the
SM.

00:26.788 --> 00:30.908
Thread Zero's load requests the value at input index zero.

00:31.688 --> 00:34.168
Load and store units handle memory instructions.

00:34.988 --> 00:39.568
A load brings a value from memory into a register, a small
fast storage

00:39.568 --> 00:41.528
location inside the SM.

00:42.208 --> 00:45.828
Registers hold the working values that arithmetic
instructions use.

00:46.448 --> 00:47.708
The request can take time.

00:48.168 --> 00:52.488
This diagram jumps ahead to when 1 .0 arrives in a
register.

00:53.168 --> 00:55.988
The input array still holds its original value.

00:55.988 --> 00:58.908
Nothing has been written to the output array yet.

01:00.349 --> 01:04.989
The multiply instruction uses the loaded value and the
multiplier two.

01:05.769 --> 01:09.989
Floating point arithmetic units perform calculations on
floating point values.

01:10.689 --> 01:15.009
Here, they produce 2 .0 and return the result to a
register.

01:15.549 --> 01:19.869
Our values use 32 -bit floating point, often called FP32.

01:20.569 --> 01:25.009
These arithmetic units are shared by threads rather than
permanently owned by

01:25.009 --> 01:25.709
thread zero.

01:26.449 --> 01:29.369
The multiply has not written the result to the output
array.

01:29.369 --> 01:30.749
That needs a store.

01:32.278 --> 01:34.238
The store is a separate instruction.

01:34.938 --> 01:39.778
Load and store units write 2 .0 from the threads result
register into output

01:39.778 --> 01:42.098
index 0 in GPU memory.

01:42.858 --> 01:44.938
The result is now in the output array.

01:45.598 --> 01:50.478
We have followed the whole value path, memory to register,
arithmetic, then

01:50.478 --> 01:51.438
register to memory.

01:53.003 --> 01:56.723
Now inspect thread one, whose input is 2 .0.

01:57.403 --> 01:59.563
Its load supplies its own working register.

02:00.283 --> 02:02.203
Thread zero's values remain separate.

02:02.683 --> 02:06.883
We are changing which values we inspect, not choosing the
GPU's execution

02:06.883 --> 02:07.283
order.

02:08.003 --> 02:11.303
A GPU can execute instructions for groups of threads
together.

02:11.823 --> 02:15.463
This picture isolates two threads to make their values
easy to follow.

02:16.979 --> 02:21.939
Thread 1's multiply uses the same depicted floating point
hardware, producing 4

02:21.939 --> 02:22.499
.0.

02:23.199 --> 02:25.619
Its store writes that result to output index 1.

02:26.239 --> 02:29.819
Each thread has its own working values, while execution
units are shared.

02:30.419 --> 02:34.699
The input and result columns identify values, not fixed
physical registers.

02:35.479 --> 02:38.579
A register can be reused after a value is no longer
needed.

02:39.219 --> 02:43.619
The boxes show hardware roles, not unit counts, or exactly
when instructions

02:43.619 --> 02:44.219
execute.

02:45.695 --> 02:46.955
Try one more case.

02:47.635 --> 02:51.255
A third thread loads 3 .0 and multiplies it by two.

02:51.955 --> 02:53.575
Its store instruction has not run.

02:54.155 --> 02:55.555
Where is the result at this point?

02:55.975 --> 02:58.215
In a working register or in the output array?

02:58.815 --> 03:00.295
Pause here and explain why.

03:04.731 --> 03:08.191
The result, 6 .0, is in a working register.

03:08.911 --> 03:11.011
The store is what writes it to the output array.

03:11.671 --> 03:14.091
That is the distinction to take back to Chapter 1.

03:14.611 --> 03:17.511
Threads describe executions with separate working values.

03:18.291 --> 03:22.051
The SM provides shared hardware for loads, arithmetic and
stores.

03:22.851 --> 03:26.911
This is a conceptual explanation, not a trace of the
kernel PyTorch

03:26.911 --> 03:27.351
selected.
